Luc Besson rejects the idea that he reinvented action cinema. He simply used to have the freedom to experiment.

&#x201C;When you don&#x2019;t have a lot of experience, you have all this freedom. You aren&#x2019;t controlled by big studios and 50 people telling you why you can&#x2019;t do something because it costs too much. For about 20 years now, people with money have taken control of cinema. They say: &#x2018;I pay, so I decide.&#x2019; No. The one who decides is the creator.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories 'Wanted' Broadway Review: Dazzling Cast Can't Elevate the Musical’s Muted Narrative Geese’s Soaring Live Show Lives Up to the Hype, With Fearless Improvisations and Boundless Energy: Concert Review

&#x201C;When Modigliani wanted to paint women with long necks and black eyes, that&#x2019;s what he did. We need to take this freedom back because we are simply killing art. We&#x2019;re turning it into something that can be sold, like yogurt or socks. There are four crucial things in the lives of men and women: Water, air, love and art. Without them, we&#x2019;re dead.&#x201D;

He added, &#x201C;Now, we have replaced everything with money. And it&#x2019;s going to kill us all.&#x201D;

Besson was speaking at Torino Film Festival , where he received the Stella delle Mole award for lifetime achievement. Earlier that day, he deadpanned, &#x201C;I saw the list of all the people who have received it, and I didn&#x2019;t understand why I hadn&#x2019;t received it too. I&#x2019;m not used to receiving awards in my own country, so I&#x2019;m happy to receive one abroad.&#x201D;

Celebrated for &#x201C;The Big Blue,&#x201D; &#x201C;L&#xE9;on: The Professional,&#x201D; &#x201C; The Fifth Element &#x201D; and &#x201C;Lucy,&#x201D; Besson was honest about his preferences.

&#x201C;Some directors are very good at making films about their neighbors &#x2013; I don&#x2019;t know how to do that. I like extraordinary stories. I make films to wander, to disappear and explore other worlds. I don&#x2019;t want to tell the story of my neighbor. I want to tell the story of a guy who drives a taxi in the 23rd century,&#x201D; he said, referencing Bruce Willis&#x2019; role in &#x201C;The Fifth Element.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;These characters are always a little bit broken by society, always on the margins. Also, I like to explore the strength of women and the weakness of men. If I was asked to make another &#x2018;Terminator,&#x2019; I&#x2019;m not interested. I like Schwarzenegger a lot, but I&#x2019;d only make that film if the Terminator sat down and said he missed his mom.&#x201D;

Besson singled out some of the actors he has enjoyed working with the most, from Gary Oldman to ex-spouse Milla Jovovich and star of &#x201C;Dogman&#x201D; Caleb Landry Jones .

&#x201C;He&#x2019;s the genius of the decade. He&#x2019;s a very simple, very sober actor. He doesn&#x2019;t go out, doesn&#x2019;t do anything. But when he&#x2019;s on set, it&#x2019;s just wonderful. He&#x2019;ll try everything,&#x201D; he revealed.

During the presser, Besson said, &#x201C;If I could make all my films with [Caleb], that would be fantastic.&#x201D;

&#x201C;As for choosing actors in general, imagine you have to cross the Atlantic in a small boat with just one other person. Choose your partner well, or it will be really hard. Now, I work with actors who are, above all, good people. I don&#x2019;t want a star surrounded by 15 people.&#x201D;

Currently finishing an animated project that he produced and which will be released next year, Besson continues to collaborate with other directors.

&#x201C;If someone else could do it as well or better than me, I don&#x2019;t need to do it myself. I couldn&#x2019;t have done &#x2018;Taken&#x2019; any better. But &#x2018;The Big Blue&#x2019; was different. My parents were divers and I spent 15 years studying dolphins, so I wasn&#x2019;t going to leave that to anyone else. &#x2018;Dogman&#x2019; was the same thing. It was a story I was afraid someone else would ruin,&#x201D; he recalled.

&#x201C;Transporter&#x201D; was another hit for him as a producer.

&#x201C;One day, an agent I knew introduced me to a young actor, and I had a drink with Jason Statham. He seemed photogenic. I told him that if I had an idea, I&#x2019;d call him. I got on a plane and came up with the idea,&#x201D; he said.

&#x201C;We wrote it in a month and financed it in three. Louis Leterrier [who directed with Corey Yuen] was an assistant who wanted to start directing. I asked: &#x2018;Are you ready?&#x2019; Hollywood doesn&#x2019;t know how to do it anymore.&#x201D;

Recalling his beginnings, he said, &#x201C;We were a group of young people who couldn&#x2019;t get films made because the big studios controlled everything. So we said: &#x2018;Fine, we&#x2019;ll make them ourselves.&#x2019; Suddenly, there were no rules. For 30 years, we did whatever we wanted.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;Unfortunately, it&#x2019;s getting harder. That&#x2019;s a real shame because the films haven&#x2019;t exactly improved, that&#x2019;s for sure.&#x201D;

Reportedly working on a new sci-fi project , Besson still advocates the kind of cinema that allows people to escape reality.

&#x201C;At 14 or 15, I lived far from the city. I could see cows outside of my window, and I felt a strong urge to get away, so I started writing about a taxi pulling up outside my house and taking me off into space. I started writing &#x2018;The Fifth Element&#x2019; at 16 because it was my personal way of escaping.&#x201D;

&#x201C;Imagination is a muscle: the more you train it, the stronger it gets. It lets you explore worlds that don&#x2019;t exist, which is wonderful, because what does exist isn&#x2019;t that great. Especially right now.&#x201D;

Chiwetel Ejiofor had his doubts when he was first approached to make the hit horror film &#x201C;Backrooms.&#x201D;

Directed by Kane Parsons , a YouTuber-turned-director, the film was produced for $10 million and has already surpassed $400 million at the global box office , becoming one of the most profitable movies of the year.

&#x201C;When I was told that Kane was only 19 years old when I was first sent the script, I thought it was very strange. He was so young! I guess I was thinking of myself at that age, and I wondered if it was plausible. Then I saw his web series [&#x2018; Backrooms &#x2019;] and it was incredible. I thought it was absolutely brilliant,&#x201D; he said during a press conference at Torino Film Festival . blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories AI's Hidden Footprint, From Quartz Mines to Kenyan Click-Workers, Bared in Taiwan Creative Content Fest Doc 'The Technological Sublime' Alien Cat Sent to Conquer Earth Goes Soft for Catnip and Canned Tuna in Taiwan Creative Content Fest Animated Series 'Operation Maomao'

In the film, Ejiofor plays a furniture store owner who discovers a mysterious portal into another dimension. Variety &#x2019;s Owen Gleiberman called it a &#x201C;creepy meditative dada horror trip in the what-is-reality? tradition of &#x2018;Eraserhead&#x2019; and &#x2018;Skinamarink.&#x2019;&#x201D;

&#x201C;I feel like there&#x2019;s a new generation of filmmakers,&#x201D; continued Ejiofor. &#x201C;They have a lot of online access, which means that a lot of the ideas are also pressure-tested. There are people who watch it and [they are] in the comments section. People aren&#x2019;t shy about telling you their opinions and thoughts.&#x201D;

He added: &#x201C;With Kane being able to communicate with millions of people and have his work interact with that number of [viewers], he was able to really refine his work in a way that wasn&#x2019;t possible a generation ago. We didn&#x2019;t have that sort of forum or access to people watching our work. He made a lot of it in his basement at home!&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;Obsession,&#x201D; made by 26-year-old Curry Barker, was another monster hit: it has made $516 million globally.

&#x201C;It&#x2019;s a very exciting time for young filmmakers and for a whole new wave of talent. I think that, in the next few years, we&#x2019;ll get to see a lot from this generation,&#x201D; said Ejiofor.

While still best known for award-winning dramas like &#x201C;12 Years a Slave,&#x201D; he has always wanted to &#x201C;explore cinema.&#x201D; Apart from &#x201C;Backrooms,&#x201D; this ambition led him to the Marvel Cinematic Universe, where he played Karl Mordo in &#x201C;Doctor Strange&#x201D; and &#x201C;Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness.&#x201D;

&#x201C;I was thrilled to join that,&#x201D; he said.

&#x201C;I&#x2019;ve always wanted to do lots of different kinds of films in my career, lots of different genres, different characters, different styles of films. I think it&#x2019;s because that&#x2019;s how I am as an audience member. I love watching all sorts of films,&#x201D; he said.

&#x201C;I enjoyed being in Marvel films, the scale and the size of them, their reach and their fan base. It continues to expand in ways that, even 10 years ago, I don&#x2019;t think we expected.&#x201D;

There&#x2019;s just one thing he asks for as an actor: his characters need to evolve.

&#x201C;It moves me. One of the hardest things for people to do is to truly change and change their inner qualities. Sometimes very epic things have to happen for this to occur. There has to be large amounts of drama, wars and conflict.&#x201D;

He said: &#x201C;I find it moving when I see characters who are in pursuit of that. In pursuit of change or growth in some way. And that&#x2019;s the kind of thing that, again, can happen across any genre.&#x201D;

Candace Cameron Bure is getting candid about the residuals she still earns from Full House .

The actress appeared on a recent episode of The McBride Rewind podcast , where she revealed that she gets "substantial" payouts from the sitcom, which ran for eight seasons from 1987 to 1995.

"They&#x2019;re substantial&#x2026; I think it&#x2019;s like quarterly," she said, noting that while some residual checks for other projects might be a few cents or dollars, the ones for Full House are "still substantial." Related Stories Lifestyle Dave Coulier Says Cancer Treatment Has Led to Changes in His Speech and Physical Appearance Movies Why Candace Cameron Bure Says Horror Films Can Lead to a "Portal" That's "Incredibly Demonic"

"I&#x2019;m always happy when I open that check and deposit it,&#x201D; Bure added. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The actress played D.J. Tanner, the eldest sister to Stephanie (Jodie Sweetin) and Michelle (Mary-Kate/Ashley Olsen), throughout the show's run. She also reprised her role in the sitcom&#x2019;s spin-off Fuller House , which ran for five seasons from 2016 to 2020.

Host Josh McBride also brought up Sweetin's appearance on the podcast in April 2026, where she shared that her residuals aren't as great as they used to be.

"I got a one-cent check the other day. No, there's no syndication anymore because it's all in streaming. Who gets paid for that? Nobody gets paid for that," Sweetin said at the time. "Sure, in my 20s, there would be money, but not reliable. You don't know how much it's going to be or how often they're going to run the show. So, sometimes you're like, 'Oh, cool. That was nice.' And then sometimes you're like, 'All right, well, there's a nice dinner out.'"

In response, Bure told McBride, "I actually saw that and she said she doesn&#x2019;t get much now. And I was like, &#x2018;Jodie, you gotta check with your agent or you gotta call your bank,' 'cause I still get residuals." blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Elsewhere on the podcast, Bure also addressed the Olsen twins' decision not to appear on the spin-off. "It was a bummer because it would have been fun to have everybody back," she said of Fuller House . "But at the same time, it wasn&#x2019;t surprising just because none of us really have stayed close with the girls."

"Not because anything went wrong or there was any bad blood. I just think they were kids. They were [filming from] 9 months old until they were 8 years old. They have very different memories than we do. Even me being 10 years older than them, I have significant, great, wonderful memories," she continued. "They were babies. They probably don&#x2019;t even remember half the stuff [we do]. They didn&#x2019;t have the attachment to the crew and the people like we did being older. And they had such a successful fashion business. They haven&#x2019;t been actors for a long time. They just haven&#x2019;t been in that part of entertainment.&#x201D;
